.SH DOCK
The dock sits left of the page: the search field between
.B \[u25D6]
and
.B \[u25D7]
on its first row (showing
.B find
while empty), then the tree of pages, with a divider on its right
edge that can be dragged to resize it.
The page narrows to make room for the dock.
.PP
The tree shows the pages around the current one: for a page of the project,
or without a page, the project's pages
.RI ( name ( section ))
and, below them in a
.B Markdown
group, its Markdown documents (by their path from the project root); for
another page, the outermost group it is in, or the page alone when it is in
none.
Names that share a prefix fold into nested groups:
.B abrt\-cli
and
.B abrt\-dump\-oops
go under
.BR abrt ,
and
.B abrt\-dump\-oops
and
.B abrt\-dump\-xorg
under
.B abrt\-dump
within it.
A prefix is made of whole tokens of the name, split at
.BR \- ", " _ ", " . ", " :
and
.B /
(so
.BR File::Spec ,
.B systemd.unit
and the project's
.B docs/guide.md
group too), and it takes at least
.B min\-group
entries (2 by default, see
.MR docv\-config 5 )
to make a group.
Groups cross sections and are case-sensitive; a page named like its group
is listed inside it.
Rows are named relative to their group, so
.B abrt\-action\-analyze\-c(1)
reads
.B c(1)
under
.BR analyze ;
a page named like its group is named relative to the group above.
Inside a group its pages come first, then its subgroups, each by name; the
top level lists them by name.
Groups unfold level by level as far as the dock has room, always around the
current page, which is selected; the tree follows the page, switching to
the group of a page in another one.
An open group is marked
.BR \[u25CB] ,
a folded one
.BR \[u25CF] ;
a branch
.RB ( \[u251C]\[u2576] ,
or
.B \[u2570]\[u2576]
for the last row of a group) connects each row to its group.
Before it, each enclosing level shows a line
.RB ( \[u2502] )
while that level continues below, a faint guide
.RB ( \[u254E] )
while only an outer level does, or nothing.
Rows wider than the dock scroll sideways.
.PP
Text in the search field without a leading
.B /
fuzzy-finds in the lines of the displayed page, in its current layout.
The page stays on screen: matched characters are highlighted in place, the
line of the selected match is shaded, and the page scrolls to the best match
while typing.
Meanwhile the lines that every term matches are counted for every page of
the manual, and the tree shows each of its pages' count at the right of the
row, its name in the
.B hit
color; a group shows how many of its pages have hits.
The text of all pages is read once, in the background, by
.B docv text\-worker
processes, and cached.
Short terms match a line of most pages.
.PP
Text after a leading
.B /
fills the tree with every page of the search path and every document of
the project, the project's first, and ranks them, each with the description from its NAME
section (for a document its first heading and paragraph) after the name,
matched against both.
The project's pages and documents come first, also ahead of better matches
elsewhere.
A match in the name counts slightly more than an equal match in the
description, so
.B /list dir
finds
.MR ls 1
by its description.
Groups without a match are left out and the others are open and ranked by
their best match, each level on its own; a group folded by hand stays folded
until the text changes.
Opening a page from the results ends the search: the field empties, the
tree shows the page's group, and the page gets the keyboard.
The descriptions are read from the pages in the background while the dock
is open, and cached.
.PP
Matching is fuzzy: the characters of each space-separated term must appear
in order, and matches score higher at word starts, in runs of consecutive
characters and with short gaps.
Every term must match.
A term in lower case ignores case; a term with an upper-case letter matches
exactly.
Results are ranked best first within the project's entries and within the
others; ties go to the shorter name, then to section order.
.SH MOUSE
.TP
wheel
Scroll 3 lines.
.TP
.RB Shift\- "wheel, horizontal wheel"
Scroll 8 columns.
.TP
drag in the text
Select text; on release it is copied to the clipboard with OSC 52.
The selection stays highlighted until the next click or key.
Indentation and trailing blanks of a line are not selected, and the copied
lines lose the indentation they share: text comes out flush left, code keeps
its relative indentation.
.TP
double-click
Select the word under the pointer (letters, digits,
.B _
and
.BR \- )
or the run of punctuation, and copy it.
.TP
triple-click
Select the paragraph: the lines with text around the pointer in blocks of
the same kind (a heading alone), and copy it.
.TP
drag a thumb, click a track
Scroll; a click on a track pages in its direction.
.TP
click a link
Follow it.
.TP
click the search field
Give it the keyboard.
.TP
hover over a row of the tree, click it
Highlight it; open the page, or fold or unfold the group.
.TP
.RB "wheel, " Shift\- "wheel over the dock"
Scroll the tree, or scroll it sideways.
.TP
drag the dock's right edge
Resize the dock.
.TP
.RB "click " \[uFF24]\[uFF2F]\[uFF23]\[uFF36] " v0.1.0"
Show the help.
.TP
point at the page's heading
Show the source and date of the page footer beside it.
.TP
.RB "click " \[u1F7AA]
Quit.
.TP
back button
Back to the previous page.
.SH SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING
With
.B syntax\-highlighting
on (the default; see
.MR docv\-config 5 ),
code examples are colored by their syntax with tree-sitter: Markdown code
blocks labeled with a language
.RB ( \(ga\(ga\(gacrystal ;
aliases such as
.BR cr ,
.BR sh ,
.BR js ,
.B py
and
.B yml
work), and the
.B .EX
examples of manual pages in a section that names a language: the
.B 3cr
and
.B 7cr
pages of
.B gen\-cr
are Crystal.
.PP
A language takes a grammar, a shared library
.BI libtree\-sitter\- name .so
or
.IB name .so\c
, and its highlight query
.IB name /highlights.scm\c
\&.
.B docv
ships neither and looks where editors install them: grammars in
.IR ~/.local/lib ,
.IR /usr/local/lib ,
.IR /usr/local/lib64 ,
.IR /usr/lib ,
.IR /usr/lib64 ,
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/docv/grammars ,
the
.B nvim
.I parser
directories
.RI ( $XDG_DATA_HOME/nvim/site/parser ,
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/\:nvim/\:lazy/\:nvim\-treesitter/\:parser )
and the
.B helix
.I runtime/grammars
directories; queries in
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/docv/queries ,
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/tree\-sitter/queries ,
the
.B nvim
.I queries
directories (also
.IR /usr/share/nvim/runtime/queries )
and the
.B helix
.I runtime/queries
directories.
A query's
.B ; inherits:
line puts the named languages' queries first.
A language without a grammar or query, or whose query the grammar rejects,
stays plain.
.PP
Captures take the
.B syntax\-
roles by their longest known dotted prefix, so
.B keyword.function
is a keyword.
An inner capture overrides the one around it; of two captures of one node
the later pattern wins, or for
.B helix
queries the earlier one.
.SH LINKS
A cross-reference
.IB name ( section )
in the text opens that page from the search path.
A Markdown link to a file opens the file, relative to the current one, at
the heading its
.BI # anchor
names; a link to
.BI # anchor
alone goes to that heading of the current document.
Anchors are GitHub's: the heading in lower case, punctuation dropped, spaces
as dashes, and
.BR \-1 ,
.BR \-2 ...
appended to repeated headings.
Following a link remembers the place, for
.BR Backspace .
.PP
A link to a URL (one with a scheme, such as
.B https:
or
.BR mailto: )
is written as a terminal hyperlink (OSC 8), for the terminal to open: most
terminals open it on a click with a modifier, such as
.B Ctrl\-Shift
in kitty, since the viewer itself takes plain clicks.
Following it in the viewer shows the URL.
.SH MARKDOWN
Markdown is read as CommonMark with the GitHub extensions: tables, task
lists, strikethrough, bare URLs as links, and alerts
.RB ( "> [!NOTE]" ).
Paragraphs fill to the width, list items hang on their bullet, number or
check box, block quotes get a bar
.RB ( \[u2502] )
per level, code blocks keep their lines unwrapped and indented, code spans
are never broken across lines, tables are
boxed as wide as the terminal with the header row ruled off, and horizontal
rules run across the width.
Headings keep their level, 1 to 6, each in its own color.
Raw HTML is dropped, keeping the text between the tags; an image shows its
description.
Markdown documents have no page header and footer.
.SH COLORS
Every element is drawn in a role, styled from the terminal's color theme.
On start, and again when the terminal reports a change of color scheme,
.B docv
asks the terminal for its colors with OSC 21 (the color control protocol of
.BR kitty ).
Once it answers, colors ramped toward its foreground or background, such as
the background of the search field, are exact and drawn in 24-bit color.
Otherwise the standard xterm colors stand in, and the color depth comes from
.B COLORTERM
and
.BR TERM .
Colors a page sets itself are kept.
Roles are restyled in
.MR docv\-config 5 .
.SH TERMINAL
.B docv
switches to the alternate screen and uses the kitty keyboard protocol, SGR
mouse reports in pixels, in-band resize, focus, visibility and color scheme
notifications, synchronized updates, OSC 21 for the color theme, OSC 52
for the clipboard, OSC 8 for hyperlinks and OSC 22 for the shape of the
mouse pointer (text, pointing hand, resize, grab).
Terminals without some of these get the legacy key encodings, cell mouse
coordinates and default colors; the clipboard write first sends an invalid
payload, which clears the clipboard in terminals that append writes and is
ignored elsewhere.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MANPATH
The system search path; see
.BR "SEARCH PATH" .
.TP
.B PATH
The directories next to which the default system path looks for pages.
.TP
.B MANWIDTH
The width of plain text output.
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
The date, in seconds since the epoch, written into the pages
.B gen\-cr
generates, for reproducible builds; today when unset.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set and not empty, no colors at all: headings bold, arguments and links
underlined, selections in reverse video.
.TP
.B COLORTERM
.B truecolor
or
.B 24bit
selects 24-bit color.
.TP
.B TERM
A name ending in
.B \-direct
selects 24-bit color, one with
.B 256color
256 colors; otherwise the 16 ANSI colors.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The base of the configuration directory,
.I ~/.config
when unset or not absolute.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
The base of the grammar and query directories (see
.BR "SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTING" ),
.I ~/.local/share
when unset or not absolute.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
The base of the cache directory,
.I ~/.cache
when unset or not absolute.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG
The locale whose page translations come first.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/docv/config.ini
The configuration, written with commented defaults on the first run; see
.MR docv\-config 5 .
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/docv/paths
The added directories, one per line; lines starting with
.B #
are comments.
.B path\-add
and
.B path\-rm
rewrite it.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/docv/text.bin
The text of all pages, for the hit counts of the tree, with each page's
modification time; only changed pages are read again.
About 200 MB for 27,000 pages.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/docv/index.tsv
The page descriptions of the page search.
A directory whose modification time is unchanged is read from it; in a
changed directory only files with a new modification time are read again.
The descriptions of the project's documents are read on every start.
.TP
.IR /etc/man_db.conf ", " /etc/manpath.config
The
.B man\-db
configuration read for the system path.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
No such page, an unreadable page or document, an invalid configuration, a
failed
.BR gen\-cr ,
or a failed
.BR path\-add ,
.B path\-rm
or file write.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
docv ls
docv 3 printf
docv \(aqprintf(3)\(aq
docv ./doc/tool.1
docv README.md
docv \-u tar
docv path\-add ~/src/tool/man
docv gen\-cr && docv Foo::Bar
